From: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] frontends/nss: leave a flow the firmware keeps flushing for
 its source interface on the host

With the source interface check on (0057) the firmware hands a packet that
arrives on another interface than its rule's to the host and flushes the
rule. That is what keeps a flow that crosses the box twice from being sent
back out of the uplink - but ECM then sees the next packet of the first
crossing and writes the rule again, the next packet of the second one
flushes it, and so on for as long as the flow runs: over a thousand rules a
second made and thrown away for one TCP stream between a guest client and
a LAN device behind the same access point, and the stream runs slower than
it does with no acceleration at all.

Such a flow cannot be accelerated: both crossings are one 5-tuple, one
connection here and one rule in the firmware. The flush message says why
the rule went (cause = the interface mismatch exception), so count those
per connection, and after net.ecm.src_interface_mismatch_limit of them in
a row, each within a second of the one before, fail the connection out the
way one whose rules never carry a packet is failed out. It stays with the
host until it ends. A single mismatch is left alone - a station that moved
to another port looks the same, once. The limit is 3; 0 turns it off.

ecm_nss_ipv4 and ecm_nss_ipv6 in debugfs get src_mismatch_flush_count
(rules flushed for this) and src_mismatch_fail_count (connections left
with the host), and the state of a connection shows its own counts.

Measured on an Archer AX55 as the access point, firmware 12.2, a guest
client and a LAN device on its two SSIDs, iperf3 for 10 s: 7600 to 20800
rules made and flushed per run before, all of them with this cause; 6 to
22 after, with the stream on the host and no slower than it was, the
router's switch table clean, and guest traffic to the WAN accelerated as
before. UDP behaves the same.

Signed-off-by: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
---
